{"name":"Glacier Valley","hex":"#e2e3d7","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"glacier","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large body of ice which flows under its own mass, usually downhill.","An area of a mountain where snow is present year-round."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/glacier"},{"term":"valley","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An elongated depression cast between hills or mountains, often with a river flowing through it.","An area which drains itself into a river."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To form the shape of a valley."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/valley"}],"entries":[{"title":"Glacier","blocks":[{"text":"A glacier is a persistent body of natural ice, a form of\nrock, that is constantly moving under its own weight. A glacier forms where the accumulation of snow exceeds its ablation over many years, often centuries. It slowly flows and deforms under stresses induced by gravity, undergoing both ductile and brittle deformation, and acquiring distinguishing surface features, such as crevasses and seracs. As a glacier moves, it abrades underlying rock and debris to create glacial landforms, such as cirques, moraines, and fjords. Although a glacier may flow into a body of water, and can include ice shelves and ice tongues, it originates only on land and is distinct from the much thinner sea ice and lake ice that form on the surface of freezing bodies of water."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Geikie_Plateau_Glacier.JPG/330px-Geikie_Plateau_Glacier.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glacier","wikidata":"Q35666"},{"title":"Valley","blocks":[{"text":"A valley is an elongated low area often running between hills or mountains and typically containing a river or stream running from one end to the other. Most valleys are formed by erosion of the land surface by rivers or streams over a very long period. Some valleys are formed through erosion by glacial ice. These glaciers may remain present in valleys in high mountains or polar areas."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Salta-VallesCalchaquies-P3140151.JPG/330px-Salta-VallesCalchaquies-P3140151.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valley","wikidata":"Q39816"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Wildspitze_seen_from_Hinterer_Brunnkogel%2C_with_visible_ascent_track_of_ski_mountaineer.jpg/500px-Wildspitze_seen_from_Hinterer_Brunnkogel%2C_with_visible_ascent_track_of_ski_mountaineer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Wildspitze_seen_from_Hinterer_Brunnkogel%2C_with_visible_ascent_track_of_ski_mountaineer.jpg/1280px-Wildspitze_seen_from_Hinterer_Brunnkogel%2C_with_visible_ascent_track_of_ski_mountaineer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Wildspitze_seen_from_Hinterer_Brunnkogel%2C_with_visible_ascent_track_of_ski_mountaineer.jpg/500px-Wildspitze_seen_from_Hinterer_Brunnkogel%2C_with_visible_ascent_track_of_ski_mountaineer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Taschachferner in the Ötztal Alps in Austria. The mountain to the left is the Wildspitze (3,768 m), the second highest in Austria","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWildspitze_seen_from_Hinterer_Brunnkogel%2C_with_visible_ascent_track_of_ski_mountaineer.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Baltoro_glacier_from_air.jpg/500px-Baltoro_glacier_from_air.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/